# Production environment reference — not a file this app reads directly.
# No values here are real secrets; every line is a placeholder plus a note
# on what it needs to become true before a real deploy. Copy the variable
# *names* into whatever your hosting provider uses for env config (Vercel/
# Render/Railway project settings, a real .env.production, etc.) — copying
# this file as-is and running it will not work, on purpose.
#
# This exists because the three deployment gates this project has
# repeatedly flagged as blocked-on-you (hosted Postgres, real API keys, a
# cron trigger) all eventually resolve to "which env vars get set, and to
# what" — this is the map from that decision to the actual code, written
# once so it doesn't need re-deriving later.

# ── Runtime ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Not optional. NODE_ENV=production is what makes lib/email.ts fail loudly
# instead of logging a raw password-reset token to your log aggregator when
# EMAIL_WEBHOOK_URL is missing (see README "What's real vs. stubbed"). If
# your host doesn't set this automatically, set it explicitly.
NODE_ENV=production
PORT=3001

# ── Database ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# schema.prisma's provider is already "postgresql", and prisma/migrations/
# already has real migration history (7 migrations as of this writing) --
# both were still open items in an earlier draft of this file, resolved once
# this project moved onto a real hosted Neon instance. Get this exact
# connection string from whichever provider you use (Neon, Supabase, RDS,
# ...); most require `?sslmode=require` for external connections. PostGIS is
# NOT required for anything this app currently does (see README) — a plain
# managed Postgres instance is sufficient.
#
# Run `npx prisma migrate deploy` (the real production-safe command; note
# it's not `prisma deploy`) against a fresh database to apply all existing
# migrations. Going forward, a schema change needs a new migration file
# generated with `npx prisma migrate dev --name <description>` against a
# real Postgres connection (migration SQL is dialect-specific), not a plain
# `db push` — this project's earlier SQLite-only phase used `db push`
# exclusively, but that path is no longer how schema changes ship.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://<user>:<password>@<host>:5432/<database>?sslmode=require"

# ── Frontend origin ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The real deployed frontend URL — CORS is locked to exactly this origin
# (see index.ts). If frontend and backend end up on different domains,
# frontend/src/api.ts's hardcoded `BASE = "/api"` (a same-origin relative
# path) also needs to become an absolute URL, or your host needs a rewrite
# rule routing /api/* to the backend — neither is wired up yet, and which
# one is right depends entirely on which hosting topology you pick.
CLIENT_URL="https://your-real-domain.example"

# ── Auth ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Generate fresh — do not reuse the dev value or anything memorable.
# `node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('hex'))"`
JWT_SECRET="<generate-a-real-random-64+-char-secret>"

# ── Third-party API keys ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# All three are optional at the code level (every adapter degrades to
# seed-only if unset) but each unlocks real, already-built functionality.
# Self-serve, free — https://developer.ticketmaster.com/
# Verified directly against Ticketmaster's own developer FAQ: the default
# quota is 2 requests/second and 5,000 requests/day (not the "5/second"
# figure that circulates in some third-party summaries). At 129 cities on
# the default hourly VOLATILE_REFRESH_MS, the scheduler alone generates
# ~3,096 calls/day (129 x 24) — under the cap, but with real margin, not
# huge margin, before any manual `npm run ingest` runs on top of it. There
# is currently no proactive daily budget guard on this adapter the way
# there is on google-places below — only reactive retry-on-429
# (lib/httpRetry.ts). Worth adding one before this is running unattended
# at full scale, using the same pattern as lib/rateLimiter.ts.
TICKETMASTER_API_KEY=""
# Self-serve, free — https://seatgeek.com/build. SeatGeek's own terms and
# rate limits couldn't be directly verified — seatgeek.com/api-terms and
# /build both block automated fetching — so no specific quota number is
# asserted here. What is confirmed: SeatGeek runs a real commercial
# partner/affiliate program, so this is not a non-commercial-only API.
SEATGEEK_CLIENT_ID=""
# Paid/metered — https://developers.google.com/maps/documentation/places/web-service
# Set a real budget alert in the Google Cloud Console itself, in addition
# to (not instead of) the app-level guard below — the app-level guard stops
# this app's own calls; a console alert catches anything the guard doesn't
# (a bug, a manual script run outside the normal path, etc.).
#
# Confirmed: googlePlaces.ts already calls places.googleapis.com/v1/... —
# the current "Places API (New)" endpoint, not the deprecated legacy API,
# so no migration is needed there. Pricing itself is per-SKU, not a flat
# "$200 credit" pool: the field mask this adapter requests (id,
# displayName, formattedAddress, location — no ratings/reviews/atmosphere)
# puts it on the Text Search *Pro* SKU, billed at $32/1,000 calls past a
# 5,000-call/month free allowance — not the ~11,000/month figure quoted in
# some summaries, which doesn't match this adapter's actual field mask.
# Re-check developers.google.com/maps/billing-and-pricing/pricing directly
# before setting a real budget — Google's SKU pricing changes.
GOOGLE_PLACES_API_KEY=""
# 161 = floor(5,000/mo Pro-SKU allowance ÷ 31, the longest possible month),
# not a placeholder — the previous value here (166 = 5,000/30) could
# theoretically total slightly over the free allowance across a real 31-day
# month if the cap were hit every single day; 161 guarantees that can never
# happen. At the default 24h STRUCTURED_REFRESH_MS below, one visited city
# can be re-fetched at most once/day, keeping this genuinely free for up to
# ~161 actively-visited priority-tier cities (the registry currently has
# 53). Raise this only if you've deliberately decided to pay $32/1,000
# calls past the free allowance — re-check
# developers.google.com/maps/billing-and-pricing before doing so, since
# Google's SKU pricing changes.
GOOGLE_PLACES_MAX_CALLS_PER_DAY=161

# ── Refresh cadence (demand-driven, not a background schedule) ──────────
# Ingestion happens on read: ensureCityFresh (services/ingestion.service.ts)
# checks these thresholds per adapter per city and only re-fetches whichever
# ones are actually stale for whoever just requested that city — nothing
# runs proactively for cities no one's visiting. STRUCTURED_REFRESH_MS
# (google-places) is 24h because that data is tagged tier "static" (90-day
# decay) — even daily is 90x more frequent than the data actually goes
# stale, kept conservative rather than stretched all the way out.
# VOLATILE_REFRESH_MS (ticketmaster/seatgeek) at 1h has enormous headroom
# against Ticketmaster's 5,000/day cap so it's left alone.
VOLATILE_REFRESH_MS=3600000
STRUCTURED_REFRESH_MS=86400000

# ── Email ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Required in production, not optional — see the NODE_ENV note above for
# what happens if this is missing when someone requests a password reset.
# Point this at whatever transactional-email provider you pick (Postmark,
# Resend, SES, ...) — lib/email.ts POSTs {to, subject, text} as JSON to
# this URL; most providers' own "generic webhook" or "send API" endpoint
# will accept that shape directly, or need a thin adapter in front of it.
EMAIL_WEBHOOK_URL="https://your-email-provider.example/send"

# ── Exchange rates (optional — nothing depends on this being set) ────────
# lib/exchangeRates.ts expects a normalized {"rate": number} JSON response
# from `${EXCHANGE_RATE_API_URL}?from=X&to=Y` — no real provider has been
# picked or verified against, so this contract is this project's own
# generic shape, not a specific vendor's actual API. Whatever you choose
# (exchangerate-api.com, Open Exchange Rates, ...) will very likely need a
# thin adapter in front of it, same as EMAIL_WEBHOOK_URL above. Leaving
# this unset is a real, supported state: GET /api/exchange-rate returns
# {rate: null}, not a guessed conversion.
#
# Separately, and not something any env var fixes: there is no "traveler's
# home currency" concept anywhere in this app yet — no user or trip field
# stores one. This endpoint can look up a rate between any two currencies,
# but nothing calls it from the UI yet, because there's no real answer to
# "convert to what" without that preference existing first. That's a
# product decision, not a technical one — see README.
#
# If exchangerate-api.com specifically is the pick: confirmed from current
# (2026) sources, its free tier is 1,500 requests/month. Whether that's
# enough depends entirely on the home-currency decision above — one fixed
# base currency against ~50 destination currencies once a day fits
# comfortably; a per-user home currency could multiply that fast.
EXCHANGE_RATE_API_URL=""
EXCHANGE_RATE_API_KEY=""

# ── Not yet an env var, but part of the same launch checklist ───────────
# - The retention sweep (`npm run sweep:stale-trips`) and the expired
#   reset-token cleanup it now also runs already have a scheduled trigger:
#   `.github/workflows/retention-sweep.yml` runs it daily via GitHub
#   Actions, reading DATABASE_URL from a repo secret. If you move hosts or
#   want a platform-native cron instead (Render/Railway), that workflow is
#   what to replace or point at the new DATABASE_URL.
# - This is a real git repository with a GitHub remote and CI already
#   wired up (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`), not a future step.
